# Google Drive backup — optional. Leave this unset and the whole feature hides
# itself: the app stays local-only and the Settings panel does not appear.
#
# This is a CLIENT ID, which is public by design — it is compiled into the
# JavaScript every visitor downloads. There is no client secret in this app and
# there must never be one: the sign-in is Google Identity Services' token flow,
# built for browser apps with no backend.
#
# To get one:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → new project
#   2. APIs & Services → Library → enable "Google Drive API"
#   3. OAuth consent screen → External → add the scopes
#        .../auth/drive.appdata   and   .../auth/userinfo.email
#      (drive.appdata is non-sensitive: it reaches one hidden folder this app
#       creates and nothing else in anybody's Drive)
#   4. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application
#   5. Authorised JavaScript origins: http://localhost:5210 and the deployed URL
#
# Set the same value in the Vercel project's environment variables.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Google Books API key — optional, and only about quota.
#
# Book search asks three catalogues at once. Google Books is the only one that
# rate-limits anonymous callers by IP, and the allowance is shared by everyone
# behind that address, so a phone on carrier NAT can arrive already over the
# line. The app handles that on its own — it caches each query, backs off for a
# minute after a 429, and says "one is rate-limiting us" instead of pretending
# the book does not exist — but a key removes the cause: quota then counts
# against the key rather than the IP (1,000 requests/day on the free tier).
#
# Unset, everything works; Google Books is simply the catalogue most likely to
# sit out a busy minute.
#
# This key is compiled into the bundle and is therefore PUBLIC. That is fine
# for the Books API and only for the Books API, and only with a restriction:
#
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → the same project as the client ID above
#   2. APIs & Services → Library → enable "Books API"
#   3. Credentials → Create credentials → API key
#   4. Edit the key → Application restrictions → Websites, and list
#        https://press.flyleaf.cc/*   and   http://localhost:5210/*
#      Then API restrictions → Restrict key → Books API.
#
# Without step 4 the key is usable by anyone who reads the JavaScript. With it,
# the worst a copied key can do is spend quota from those two origins.
#
# Set the same value in the Vercel project's environment variables.
VITE_GOOGLE_BOOKS_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The tip jar — optional, and off unless BOTH halves are configured.
#
# The row in Settings appears only when this is exactly "1". The page half
# holds no keys at all: amounts are checked, checkouts opened, and payments
# verified by api/tip.ts, a Vercel function that reads the Paystack SECRET key
# from the server environment. The secret never appears in this file, in the
# repo, or in the bundle.
#
# To switch it on, set in the Vercel project's environment variables:
#   VITE_TIP_JAR=1              (build-time — shows the row)
#   PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=sk_...  (server-only — never prefixed VITE_)
#   PAYSTACK_CURRENCIES=NGN     (optional — comma list of currencies the
#                                Paystack account can settle, e.g. NGN,USD)
#   TIP_RETURN_ORIGIN=          (optional — where Paystack sends supporters
#                                back to; defaults to the production URL)
#
# Unset here, the dev server hides the row, which is right: localhost has no
# api/ to answer it.
VITE_TIP_JAR=
